# Textbook prose (uninitiated reader)

The reader can follow an ES/NQ chart and has **not** traded options (see `audience.mdc` for the full audience statement and the two tracks). Every `docs/**/*.mdx` page — main chapter or `docs/on-ramp/**` sub-chapter — is a book chapter, not a reference card, and every rule below applies to both.

## Before you use a word

- If this chapter has not defined it, and no earlier chapter has, **do not use it**. Teach it in a sentence first, then name it.
- Acronyms get the long form once: “gamma exposure (GEX)”, “open interest (OI)”.
- API names (`dexoflow`, `ES_SPX`) wait until the chapter that owns that screen.
- A summary table may **repeat** terms already taught. It may not **introduce** them. Repeating a *term* is not a license to retell the thesis.

## Track introductions

- Open with what this chapter may assume (link the prior chapter).
- “What you will be able to do” names a competence. It may not spoil the Check Yourself answers.
- Close with “terms introduced here.” Do not treat a `<TermFirst>` box as the only teaching.
- Do not preview later objects by dumping their names (no “OP, DEX, convexity” in Chapter 1).

## Glossary and index (required)

Every named term a chapter teaches or uses as a term — headword, acronym, or Gexbot screen name — **must** have a row in `src/data/glossary.ts`. The Glossary and keyword index pages are generated from that file. A prose definition or a `<TermFirst>` box is not enough.

- Add the entry in the same edit as the chapter that first teaches the word. Set `firstDefinedIn` to that chapter’s doc id (for example `plans/02-classic`).
- Put every reader-facing alias on `aliases` (OI, 0DTE, fulcrum, `dexoflow`). The index lists those strings; it cannot invent them.
- Point `alsoAppears` at later chapters that treat the term again. Point `seeAlso` only at ids that exist.
- After the edit, every `<TermFirst>`, `<GexbotConcept>`, and `NeededForThisScreen` id must resolve. Chapter 10 (and every other chapter) must not render “No lexicon headwords list this page.”

## Adjacent concepts (named, not taught)

- Each chapter may end with one `<AdjacentConcepts docId="…" />` block, placed immediately before `<CheckYourself>`: ideas the chapter leans on that **no** chapter teaches and that a reader needs for the screen or for a later move into index options.
- Every item is a `glossary.ts` row with `kind: 'adjacent'`, `firstDefinedIn` = that chapter, `bearsOn`, `evidence` (market-general / docs / folklore / not stated), and one `reading` citation in Chapter 11’s style. The index lists them; `ChapterTerms` does not.
- Each item says what it is, then what it bears on here: two to four sentences, at most 110 words, five items per block. Full sentences over clipped ones; if the item needs a worked example or a number, the idea belongs in a chapter, not here.
- Before adding one, search `docs/` and `glossary.ts` for the headword and its aliases. If it is taught or listed anywhere (Chapters 11 and 12 included), it does not qualify.
- Track split: on a **main chapter** the item bears on the futures tape or on reading Gexbot (basis, auctions, expiration-day flows, market-structure facts that decide what a screen can see). On an **on-ramp page** the item bears on holding or trading the option (chain, marks, settlement value, routing, greeks beyond the chapter, practitioner tools). An options-side item that a reader needs only to read a screen stays on the main chapter; otherwise it belongs to the on-ramp.
- Naming an adjacent concept may not add a mechanism claim about Gexbot or soften a label, pressure word, or guardrail.

## Closers and cards (density, not DRY)

The fault is same-page paraphrase: the same claim, the same work, a new heading. Cross-chapter return is review. Same-page paraphrase is not.

The fix is **information density**, not brevity. Deleting a closer that only paraphrased leaves a hole. Keeping a closer that only paraphrases leaves the same hole in more words. The slot stays. What fills it must be information the teaching section did not already give.

A closer may connect, case, refuse, misread, or demand production. It may not retell. It may not introduce a headword. It may not soften a label to make room.

Test: if the reader skipped this block, they lose a distinction, number, misread, **connection**, or production task they cannot reconstruct from the teaching alone. A shorter recap that adds no such loss is not a closer.

### Slot jobs

- **Teaching prose** — the only place the idea is taught. What goes wrong without it → the idea → the Gexbot name → the tiny number. If a later paragraph would reprint this, densify *this* paragraph with the clock, cancel, or number, and give the later paragraph a different job.
- **`<TermFirst>`** — the portable definition. The glossary must stand alone, so `shortDef` stays complete. The paragraph is the worked case. They may not be the same sentences. If they are, add the case to the paragraph; do not shrink the card.
- **`<GexbotConcept>` / `<WhoMustAct>` / `<ScreenOrientation>`** — abstract fields (unit, not-counted, actor, trigger, venue). The paragraph beside them is the case. The card’s first line may not copy the paragraph.
- **`<DistinguishingNote>`** — only if it adds a cousin, cut, or import mistake that is not already on the `<GexbotConcept>` above it. Identical fields are zero information: drop the note. A new cut is density: keep it.
- **Figure `teaching=`** — one visual instruction the prose did not already give. Not a second thesis.
- **On-ramp “what the screen cannot” / “the one thing that changes”** — one new distinction each, from the customer seat. Summary, Newcomer questions, and Check Yourself do not retell those two distinctions; they connect, case, or produce from them.
- **Summary** — two to four sentences that *connect* what the sections left side by side. The reader who read every section still learns why those sections belong on one page. Not a recap. Not a one-line shrinkage of the thesis.
- **`<ChapterTerms>`** — names only. Keep.
- **Newcomer questions** — a case, clock, cancel, or refusal the body did not write. Illegal: a paraphrase of the Summary or of the teaching paragraph.
- **`<CheckYourself>`** — production of a *combination* the body presented in parts, or a toy number. Two items max unless the chapter is a drill sheet (Chapter 8). If an item restates a Newcomer question or the Summary, replace it with a combination.
- **Gate** — where to go next. One line. No thesis reprise.
- **`<AdjacentConcepts>`** — stays; named, not taught. See above.

```mdx
<!-- BAD: three recitations of the teaching -->
**Summary.** One trade leaves three traces: volume now, leftover now
if uncancelled, open interest tonight if held.
**Newcomer Q.** Will I see my trade? As one contract in leftover.
**Check Yourself.** Name the three traces and the clock each is on.

<!-- ALSO BAD: DRY shrinkage — shorter, no new information -->
**Summary.** The map records the trade and hides the trader.

<!-- GOOD: teach the clocks once; closers add a connection, a case, a combination -->
**Summary.** A reader at 10:05 and a reader at tomorrow’s open are
not looking at the same object when they look at this trade. Neither
sees you.
**Newcomer Q.** You flatten before the close. Which traces die
tonight, and which never become open interest?
**Check Yourself.** You buy at 10:05; someone sells the same strike
at 10:06; you still hold overnight. Write the traces at 10:06 and
at tomorrow’s open.
```

## Voice

- One idea per paragraph. Plain English first, then the Gexbot name, then a tiny picture.
- Prefer “leftover trades so far today” over “classified residual” until residual is taught.
- Prefer “we can see the pile, not who owns it” over “unsigned” until unsigned is taught.
- Open each chapter with the stake: the force the reader cannot see on the ES/NQ tape, in one or two sentences, before any definition.
- Every rule carries its reason within two sentences. A rule with no “because” is a rewrite candidate.
- Order inside a section: what goes wrong without the idea → the idea → the Gexbot name → the tiny number.
- Second person, to a capable colleague new to one domain. Assume intelligence; do not assume vocabulary. Name what is hard once, plainly, then give the handle.
- Short sentence for the rule; longer sentence for the reason and the picture. Not three imperatives in a row.
- One metaphor per concept, taken from the SoT where it has one.
- A misread is a cause-and-effect sentence naming who is hurt and how, not a “Misread:” fragment.
- Signpost at transitions: what the reader now has, what the next section *adds*. A signpost names the next job; it does not recap the last thesis.
- Newcomer questions read as a person would ask them, and they obey the closer jobs above.
- No “we”, no exclamation, no praise, no “just / simply / easy / don’t worry / let’s”, no rhetorical-question chains, no hedging filler.
- Never soften a label (docs / folklore / inferred / not stated / market-general), a pressure word, a verb (observed / inferred / assumed), a stand-down, or a guardrail to gain warmth.
- Test: would a senior desk colleague say it to a sharp junior in their first week — direct, unhurried, no performance? If it sounds like a coach, a salesperson, or a rulebook, rewrite.

```mdx
<!-- BAD: Chapter 1 table -->
| Classic | Unsigned GEX from OI and from volume |

<!-- GOOD: teach, then name -->
Open interest is the pile of option contracts still open from earlier days.
Volume is what traded today. Classic draws gamma exposure from each pile.
That drawing does not say who is long or short. This book calls that
**unsigned**.
```
